<BaseLayout title="LV3 (Taichong) — Great Surge Point" description="Learn how to locate and stimulate LV3 (Taichong) — the Great Surge point. Benefits include stress relief, headache relief, and Liver Qi stagnation support." breadcrumbs={[{ label: "Home", href: "/" }, { label: "Points", href: "/points" }, { label: "LV3 (Taichong)", href: "/points/lv3-taichong-acupressure-point" }]} articleSchema={true}>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-12 lg:px-6">
    <h1 class="mb-4">LV3 (Taichong) — Great Surge</h1>
    <p class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 text-lg mb-8">LV3, known as Taichong or Great Surge, is the source point of the Liver meridian. It is a powerful point for relieving stress, anger, headaches, and Liver Qi stagnation.</p>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Location</h2>
    <img src="/images/lv3-location.webp" alt="LV3 acupressure point location on the top of the foot" class="w-full max-w-sm mx-auto rounded-xl shadow-sm" />
    <p class="mb-3">LV3 is on the top of the foot, in the depression between the first and second metatarsal bones, about 1 cun (one thumb-width) from the web of the toes. It lies on the Liver meridian.</p>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Benefits &amp; Uses</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-3 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li><strong>Stress and irritability:</strong> LV3 is the go-to point for releasing pent-up frustration, anger, and emotional tension.</li>
      <li><strong>Headaches:</strong> It is especially effective for headaches originating from stress or high blood pressure.</li>
      <li><strong>Menstrual discomfort:</strong> LV3 helps regulate menstrual flow and eases cramps by moving Liver Qi.</li>
      <li><strong>High blood pressure:</strong> Regular stimulation may help lower elevated blood pressure related to stress.</li>
      <li><strong>Digestive issues:</strong> It relieves nausea, acid reflux, and abdominal bloating linked to emotional stress.</li>
    </ul>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">How to Massage LV3</h2>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li>Sit comfortably and place your foot on your opposite knee.</li>
      <li>Locate LV3 between the first and second toes on the top of the foot.</li>
      <li>Use your thumb to apply firm, deep pressure in a circular motion.</li>
      <li>Hold for 1— minutes, then switch to the other foot.</li>
      <li>Repeat daily, especially during stressful periods.</li>
    </ol>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Science &amp; Research</h2>
    <p class="mb-3">A 2019 study in <em>Journal of Integrative Medicine</em> found that LV3 acupressure significantly reduced blood pressure and heart rate in hypertensive patients.<sup><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31606327/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1</a></sup> Research in <em>Evidence-Based Complementary and Alternative Medicine</em> showed that LV3 stimulation reduced cortisol levels and perceived stress in college students.<sup><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32180847/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2</a></sup></p>
    <p class="mb-3">A 2021 fMRI study in <em>Brain Imaging and Behavior</em> demonstrated that LV3 acupressure modulates the amygdala and prefrontal cortex, key brain areas for emotional regulation.<sup><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32989665/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">3</a></sup></p>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Precautions</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-3 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li>Avoid pressing too hard on the top of the foot ?the bones are delicate and the area is sensitive.</li>
      <li>Do not use if you have a foot injury, gout, or fracture in the area.</li>
      <li>Avoid during pregnancy as LV3 is considered a contraindicated point.</li>
      <li>Stop if you experience sharp pain or numbness.</li>
    </ul>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Related Points</h2>
    <p class="mb-3">LV3 pairs well with <a href="/points/li4-hegu-acupressure-point">LI4 (Hegu)</a> ?together they are known as the "Four Gates" for moving Qi throughout the body. For stress-related headaches, combine with <a href="/points/gb20-fengchi-acupressure-point">GB20 (Fengchi)</a>.</p>
  </section></article>
</BaseLayout>
